    },
    {
      "layout": "Q",
      "strengths": "Whole keys and single-line headers support lookup. Its wider English column keeps the empty-cart string on one line, producing the shortest scrolling layout shown. Cells remain at most two lines tall.",
      "faults": "The English column extends beyond the initial viewport, so some English text is cut by the viewport edge until the reader scrolls. One measured break occurs inside a word, and the table requires extensive horizontal scrolling.",
      "spatial_logic": "It spends 20 more pixels on English than P, largely at the expense of Chinese and Japanese, to remove a consequential row wrap."
    },
    {
      "layout": "R",
      "strengths": "Whole identifiers and single-line headers preserve the table's lookup structure. It is the narrowest scrolling layout shown, and cells remain at most two lines tall.",
      "faults": "The narrower English column wraps the search string as well as the empty-cart string, increasing table height. One measured break occurs inside a word. Its small reduction in horizontal scrolling offers little compensation for the extra wrapping.",
      "spatial_logic": "It conserves width chiefly in English while retaining generous key and Japanese columns."
    },
    {
      "layout": "S",
      "strengths": "All five columns are visible at once without horizontal scrolling. Its relatively wide key and English columns give those fields more room than U does.",
      "faults": "Identifiers and words break repeatedly, Japanese and Arabic headers wrap, and the long Japanese message reaches ten lines. The resulting tall rows and narrow text fragments make lookup and cross-language comparison difficult.",
      "spatial_logic": "It favors keys and English within the fixed viewport, leaving especially narrow columns for Chinese and Japanese."
    },
    {
      "layout": "T",
      "strengths": "Keys and headers stay whole, and cells remain at most two lines tall. More room for Japanese may improve its phrase breaks compared with the narrower Japanese columns.",
      "faults": "English wraps the empty-cart string, so the table is taller than Q. One measured break occurs inside a word, and most translations are initially off-screen.",
      "spatial_logic": "It shifts space toward Japanese while keeping a full-width key column and moderate widths for the other languages."
    },
    {
      "layout": "U",
      "strengths": "All columns and headers are visible at once, with every header on one line. It is shorter than S, and its wider Japanese and Arabic columns reduce some of the extreme fragmentation seen there.",
      "faults": "Keys break at arbitrary points across several lines, undermining identifier lookup. Words still break frequently, and long translations form narrow vertical stacks.",
      "spatial_logic": "It redistributes space from keys and English to Japanese and Arabic, improving header fit and row height while sacrificing identifier continuity."
    }
  ],
  "insights": "The scrolling layouts preserve keys and keep rows manageable, but the first viewport shows little beyond English and cuts some English text at the viewport edge. Q shows that a modest width increase can eliminate an entire row of wrapping; R shows that a small width saving can have a disproportionate height cost. The fitting layouts expose all languages together, but the available width forces severe fragmentation, especially of identifiers. Within that class, U's language allocation gives a more usable overall result than S, although neither makes key lookup comfortable. The proposed widths are estimates and need rendered verification, particularly for the remaining measured word break and phrase boundaries.",
